ped  a unique opportunity for many to use big  data analysis tools for the first time  which otherwise they  would not have an opportunity to use and  many users reported a measurable  increase in their understanding of  technical bioinformatics terminology  and understanding of how to practically  apply such methods  to different types of data sets and  challenges  and we're also excited to see many  undergraduate as well as  graduate students provide feedback for  the asynchronous training materials  the analytical pipelines that they've  used and participation in webinars that  was offered in collaboration with lbrn  and i think that this is especially  relevant because we do have such a  diverse population  of students that have very different  backgrounds and very different levels of  exposure to research  using bioinformatics and data science  and this includes both undergraduate  students and graduate students  throughout the elbow and primarily  undergraduate institutes  institutions as well as faculty that  were able to  learn about different aspects of  bioinformatics and even use  some of those tools in their own  research  so that's all i have for today thank you  for  inviting me to share this  all right uh thanks elia  um if there are any questions for any  either of the either chris or elia now's  a good time  the floor is open for questions if you  have any in there otherwise we'll  transition to dr  lee from there the only comment that we  make remiss is that uh  all new funded projects will go through  review  by both cores and for by our  statistician  before they are awarded uh and there  will be  a session where we're going to be  reviewing  whether the appropriate tools have been  used or what else can be done to  help them move forward as we move  forward  and of course the same issue would be  with new applications coming in  they will be reviewed by both cores and  especially on the statistical side to  make sure that  ahead of the game we h